      "chapters": [
        {
          "heading": "The Last Silence",
          "body": [
            "The wind above the city of Aelindra carried the scent of salt and something else — something metallic and wrong, like the taste of a word on the tip of a tongue that refused to be spoken. On the highest terrace of the Obsidian Spire, Arch-Scribe Lyrianne pressed her palms against the cool stone and felt the city's heartbeat stutter. The thrum that had been constant for seven thousand years — the pulse that every mage born in Aelindra learned to feel before they learned to speak — was faltering.",
            "Below, in the laboring district, a mother held her child close and sang a lullaby that her grandmother had taught her. She did not know it was one of the ancient songs of Aeloria, a melody older than her civilization. In the military barracks, Captain Dravos of the Aelindran Legionary sharpened his sword. He had served for twenty years. He had never seen the fear in his soldiers' eyes like this. In the catacombs beneath the city, a child named Edric explored places he should not have been. He found a chamber older than anything he had seen, with symbols carved into stone that seemed to shift when he wasn't looking. There was a crystal sphere on a shelf. It pulsed with a faint, sickly light. He reached for it.",
            "On the terrace, Lyrianne watched the seven divine spires flicker in unison. For the first time in recorded history, all seven lights dimmed simultaneously. The darkness between them expanded. She heard a voice — not with her ears, but with something deeper, something in the marrow of her bones. It was not speaking to her. It was speaking to something else, something vast and ancient that existed below the foundation of the world. She understood one word: Enough."
