            "What is certain is that before the Sundering, before the rise of the great kingdoms, there were others. The evidence is scattered across the continents. In the deep jungles of Sylvaneth, beneath the roots of the Worldheart Tree, archaeologists have uncovered stonework of impossible precision — blocks fitted so perfectly that not even a knife blade can be inserted between them. In the Kharzul Wastes, deep within abandoned strongholds, there are chambers that the dwarves themselves do not build and cannot explain — spaces where the stone is not stone at all, but something harder, smoother, and slightly warm to the touch.",
            "In the coastal regions of Aurelia, divers have found structures submerged in waters so deep that no natural explanation can account for their presence. Some appear to have been cities. Others seem to have been something else entirely — monuments, perhaps, or machines, or temples to gods that no longer exist.",
            "The most unsettling discovery was made in the Year of the Silent Moon, 847, by an expedition sponsored by the Solmere Academy of Historical Inquiry. Deep beneath the ruins of what would later become the city of Thornwall, they found a sealed chamber. The seal was not locked. It was not barred. It was built to be opened from the inside. Inside, they found a library — thousands of tablets, carved from a material that resembled obsidian but was somehow more durable, covered in a script that no living scholar could read. Several scholars who handled the tablets reported vivid dreams of cities that burned with silver fire and skies that were not blue but a deep, impossible violet.",
            "The most disturbing detail was this: the chamber showed no signs of the Sundering. No fractures, no scorch marks. It had been sealed calmly, deliberately, by someone who expected to return. But no one returned. Master Historian Cael Vorthan noted in his report: 'This chamber was abandoned long before the Sundering. Whoever built it chose to leave, sealing their knowledge behind them, and never came back. The question is not what happened to them. The question is why they left.'"
