          "heading": "The Aelindran Dominion",
          "body": [
            "The Aelindran Dominion did not begin as an empire. It began as a village. In the Age of Settling, approximately nine thousand years before the present day, a group of humans migrated north from the warmer southern coasts of Aurelia, drawn by a fertile river valley where the Oren River met the sea. The village was called Aelindra, meaning 'first dawn.' Nothing about it suggested it would become the center of a civilization that would endure for three thousand years.",
            "But the founders of Aelindra had something other tribes did not: a memory. Their oral traditions, preserved by a caste of storytellers known as the Remembrancers, contained detailed accounts of the civilizations that had come before. They spoke of the Builders and the Silent Ones with reverence and caution. They knew the locations of ancient wells that still functioned, ancient granaries that still held grain. While other tribes built from scratch, the Aelindrans built upon what they found.",
            "By the end of the first millennium, the Aelindran Dominion had expanded to encompass most of western Aurelia. Its greatest achievement was the Academy of the Eternal Flame, founded in the year 412 by a coalition of mages, priests, and scholars who believed that knowledge itself was sacred. The Academy was built atop the ruins of a Silent One structure — a vast, open chamber that seemed to amplify magical resonance.",
            "But the Academy's most important contribution was not a spell or a scripture. It was a question. The Academy's founder, the mage Thessaly Vorn, proposed that the seven gods of the Elysian Circle were not the first divine beings to exist. She argued, based on her study of ancient ruins and magical artifacts, that there had been others — gods whose names had been forgotten, whose aspects had been absorbed into the seven, whose followers had been erased from history. The proposal was denounced as heresy. Thessaly was stripped of her position and exiled. She died three years later. But the idea had taken root."
