          "heading": "The Weight of the Incomplete Circle",
          "body": [
            "To understand the Sundering, one must understand the Elysian Circle — or rather, the many ways in which the gods have been understood. The official doctrine holds that seven gods created the world together, each contributing their aspect: Solthar brought light and justice. Nyxara brought shadow and freedom. Terravox brought earth and endurance. Maelithir brought sea and storm. Ignareth brought war and courage. Aeloria brought nature and life. Arcane brought the fundamental laws of magic.",
            "The druids of Sylvaneth tell a different story. They believe the world was not created by the gods but shaped by them from a pre-existing foundation — the work of the Silent Ones, or perhaps of the world itself. The dwarven clans believe Terravox was the first god, the foundation upon which all others were built. The orcish clans hold that the gods are not separate beings but aspects of a single divine consciousness — a consciousness that is itself part of the world rather than separate from it.",
            "The beastkin tribes hold yet another view: the gods are not cosmic beings but local ones, spirits of specific places and phenomena elevated to divine status by human worship. This view suggests that the Sundering may have damaged not just the world but the gods themselves.",
