        {
          "heading": "The First Signs of Disaster",
          "body": [
            "The years leading up to the Sundering were not marked by a single dramatic event but by a slow accumulation of wrongness. The priests of Maelithir noticed the tides shifting — not dramatically, but unmistakably. Brother Caelum, stationed on the island monastery of Deepwater, wrote: 'The tide is hesitating. It moves forward, then pauses, as if uncertain of itself. I have studied the tides for forty years. The sea does not hesitate. The sea does not doubt.'",
            "The priests of Aeloria reported that migration patterns were shifting, that animals were restless and agitated, that the great forests were showing signs of stress. The mages who studied magical currents detected that ambient magic was becoming erratic — spells that had been reliable for centuries began to fail unpredictably. And then there were the dreams. Across the continent, people began dreaming of a vast, dark space — a void that was not empty but full. A voice calling from the darkness. A city of crystal and shadow. A presence on a throne, waiting — patiently, eternally — for something.",
            "The dreams were not limited to mages or priests. They affected everyone — farmers, merchants, soldiers, children. The Aspect Seekers recognized the dreams as a resonance, spreading through the magical currents. The High Council convened an emergency session. All seven high priests gathered in the Chamber of Echoes. The priest of Solthar, Elara Dawnmantle, argued the crystal must be destroyed. The priest of Nyxara, Vael Shadowmantle, argued they should listen. 'I propose we open the crystal and hear what Kaelgorath has to say. I propose we treat this as a dialogue rather than a crisis.' The chamber erupted.",
            "In the end, the crystal was brought to the Obsidian Spire. The seven high priests surrounded it, channeling the power of their respective gods. The crystal shattered — but not the way they intended. A wave of energy erupted, overwhelming everyone present. The lights of the divine spires flickered violently. And then, silence. The dreams stopped. The anomalies ceased. For a moment, it seemed the crisis had been averted. But the priests who had been present reported something troubling: in the instant of the shattering, each had felt not seven presences in the divine currents, but eight. The eighth had been there all along. They had simply been unable to perceive it. Until now."
