        {
          "heading": "The Seven Days After",
          "body": [
            "The immediate aftermath of the Sundering was silence. Not the violent silence of the storms, but a deep, hollow silence — the silence of a world that has lost something fundamental. The magical currents were disrupted. The divine presences were dimmed, confused, uncertain. The seven gods did not speak. Kaelgorath did not speak. The world simply lay still.",
            "The geography of Elysium had been transformed. The continent of Aurelia was split by a massive chasm. The coastline had shifted. Mountains had risen where there had been plains. Rivers had changed course. New islands had appeared in the sea. Vethmoor had been rent by earthquakes that collapsed entire mountain ranges. Sylvaneth had seen its ancient trees burn in magical fires. The Kharzul Wastes had been buried under additional layers of sand and ash. Nightreach had simply vanished from some maps.",
            "The civilizations of the Ancient Age were destroyed. Not all at once — some cities survived the initial cataclysm. But the infrastructure that had sustained them — trade networks, agricultural systems, knowledge networks, magical institutions — was shattered. The Academy of the Eternal Flame, which had stood for three thousand years, collapsed into the fissure. Its library, one of the greatest in the world, was lost.",
            "The survivors told stories. Of the scholar who tried to save the library and was consumed by the collapsing building. Of the priest who prayed to his god and heard nothing. Of the soldier who tried to hold the line and was swept away. Of the child separated from her family, wandering the ruins alone. Some of these stories became legends. Some became myths. Some were forgotten."
