          "heading": "The Last Scribe",
          "body": [
            "The most enduring story from the immediate aftermath is the tale of the Last Scribe of Aelindra. According to the most common version, he was a young scholar who had been studying in the Academy when the Sundering began. As the city collapsed, he gathered as many texts as he could carry and fled into the catacombs. He spent three days underground, copying the most important passages onto stone walls, wooden planks, his own clothing.",
            "When he emerged, the city was gone. But he had the copies. He carried them as he wandered the shattered landscape, searching for other survivors. He found them — small groups, scattered and lost. The Last Scribe shared his copies, and the survivors read them and argued about them. In those first years, the act of reading and interpreting texts became a form of survival.",
            "He eventually settled in the ruins of Solmere and established a small school. He taught his students that history was not a fixed narrative but a living conversation, that the same event could be understood in multiple ways, that the truth was often more complicated than any single account suggested. He died in the Year of the Quiet Earth, at the age of thirty-two.",
            "The most famous passage from his unfinished history reads: 'We built a world that we believed would last forever. We believed this because the gods told us so, or because we told ourselves so, or because we could not imagine any alternative. We were wrong. The world did not last. It was not destroyed by an enemy or a disaster or a god's wrath. It was destroyed by a wound that we inflicted on ourselves and then refused to heal. We are the inheritors of a broken world. It is our responsibility to decide whether we will continue to break it, or whether we will begin to heal it.'"
