          "heading": "The Forging of the Concord",
          "body": [
            "The Kingdom of Solmere was founded by Marcus Dawnmantle, a former soldier who governed initially as a military dictator but established a council of advisors drawn from guilds, religious orders, and academic institutions — a deliberate echo of the old Dominion's High Council. The Kingdom of Thornwall was founded by Lady Blackthorn's descendants, built on the opposite side of the great chasm from Solmere.",
            "Thornwall's relationship with Kaelgorath is complicated. Its founders carried the heritage of Kaelgorath's worshippers, but the Sundering had destroyed the world he was supposed to have healed. The city's theologians spent centuries debating: was Kaelgorath a savior betrayed by the other gods? A destroyer disguised as a healer? The answer that emerged held that Kaelgorath was a necessary part of the divine order, but his return had been premature — the world had been broken not by the act of restoration but by the fact that the seven gods had not been prepared to accept it.",
            "In the Year of the United Flame, approximately eight hundred years after the Sundering, the Dawnbound Concord was formed as a formal alliance between Solmere, Thornwall, and smaller states sharing their theological orientation. The Concord's official theology holds that the Elysian Circle remains the legitimate authority, and that Kaelgorath's return was a 'catastrophic miscalculation.' The eighth aspect is acknowledged but its restoration is deemed too dangerous to attempt.",
            "The Concord is governed by a council of seven representatives, headed by a Dawnkeeper elected for ten-year terms. Its military, the Order of the Dawnsworn, is the most powerful on Aurelia. The Temple of Solthar, the largest religious organization in the world, maintains temples in every major city. But beneath this impressive edifice, cracks are visible: the worship of Kaelgorath is forbidden, the study of ancient texts is discouraged, and Revenants are forced to submit to regular spiritual evaluations."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The Rising of the Pact",
          "body": [
            "The Free Cities of the East had a different origin. They were not founded by a single leader or ideology but by a diverse collection of survivors who rejected the authority of both Solmere and Thornwall. Some were disillusioned citizens of the Dominion. Others were followers of Kaelgorath driven from their homes. Still others were members of minority races — orcish clans, beastkin tribes, Revenants — who had been marginalized by the human-dominated kingdoms.",
            "The formation of the Duskward Pact was a gradual process — treaties, alliances, and agreements binding the Free Cities into a loose coalition. The Pact had no monarch, no parliament, no centralized government. It was a network of mutual defense agreements, trade pacts, and shared theological commitments, held together by conviction that the old world's authorities were fundamentally untrustworthy.",
            "The Pact's capital, Ashka Vor, was not built but discovered — an ancient ruin of the Silent Ones, buried beneath the earth. The city's architecture was unlike anything the explorers had seen: towers of twisted stone, bridges spanning chasms without visible supports, chambers humming with resonance. The city's foundations are laced with chambers and passages never fully explored, some containing artifacts still active despite the collapse of the civilizations that created them.",
            "The Duskward Pact's theology is as diverse as its politics. The most common orientation is Nyxarism — the worship of Nyxara, goddess of shadows, secrets, and freedom. But the Pact also includes worshippers of Ignareth, followers of Aeloria, devotees of Arcane, and those who worship Kaelgorath openly, defiantly, in defiance of both the Concord's prohibition and the theological uncertainties surrounding the eighth god."
          ]
