        {
          "heading": "The Forgotten Ruins and the Secrets Beneath",
          "body": [
            "The ruins of the Ancient Age are everywhere. In the forests of Sylvaneth, towers of white stone rise above the canopy. In the mountains of the Kharzul Wastes, abandoned strongholds sprawl like the bones of dead giants. In the coastal regions of Aurelia, submerged structures lie beneath the waves. What the ruins share is a sense of age — even the most recently built structures of the Ancient Age feel ancient, but the older structures feel something else entirely: timeless, as if they were not built but simply are.",
            "The most famous ruins include the Spire of Echoes — a tower of black stone in the heart of the great chasm that produces sounds not heard but felt in the bones and mind — and the Hollow Throne, a chair of light-absorbing stone in an abandoned stronghold that the dwarven clans refuse to enter. Most disturbing are the ruins that show no signs of the Sundering — chambers sealed from the inside, corridors bricked up, entrances blocked with mechanisms that can only be opened from within.",
            "Beneath the surface, there are things that should not exist. The dwarven clans call the deepest layer the Vaeth-Core — the Breath Beneath. The stone there is too hard to mine, the air carries a resonance that causes hallucinations, and there are sounds — deep, rhythmic vibrations like the beating of an immense heart. The Elven druids call it the Vaeth-Orin — the Breath Before. The beastkin tribes describe similar phenomena in their sacred caves.",
            "A scholar named Lyria Voss, descendant of Aldric Voss, found an object in the deep catacombs beneath Aelindra: a seed, no larger than a fist, pulsing with a faint, rhythmic light. The light pulsed in the pattern of the Elysian Circle, but with an eighth pulse — fainter but unmistakable. Her notes end with: 'The seed is not a device. It is a seed — something planted, something growing, something that will one day bloom. If the world is older than the gods, then the gods are not the architects of the world. They are the gardeners. And the seed is the garden itself.' The seed was lost during the Sundering. Its current location is unknown."
          ]
