        {
          "heading": "The Void Herald",
          "body": [
            "The Void Herald has appeared in Elysium at least three times in recorded history, and possibly more. The first appearance was in the Year of the Divided Moon, in the city of Thornwall — a tall, robed figure whose face was obscured by shadow, standing on the city walls for three hours before vanishing. The second was in the Year of the Fading Light, in the forests of Sylvaneth, where hunters followed a figure that always remained at the same distance regardless of how fast they moved.",
            "The third appearance was in Ashka Vor, in the sealed chamber with the mural of the family fleeing a burning city. A construction worker saw the figure standing before the mural, hand raised as if touching the painted surface. The worker shouted. The figure turned toward him. He described the face as 'not a face' — a blank surface that absorbed light like the surface of a dark pool. The figure vanished. The sealed chamber locked itself again.",
            "The Dawnbound Concord officially regards the Void Herald as a 'spiritual phenomenon' associated with the Sundering's disruption. The Duskward Pact's interpretations vary: a servant of Kaelgorath, Kaelgorath himself in diminished form, or an entirely separate entity. The orcish clans call it Vaeth'Kor — the Watcher Before — a principle of observation, witnessing, remembering. They believe it has existed since before the gods.",
            "Ancient texts from the Silent Ones reference a matching figure, with a single repeated phrase: 'The Watcher remembers what the world forgets.' If the Void Herald existed before the Sundering, it may possess knowledge no living person holds — what happened to the Silent Ones, why the world has been broken before, what is coming next. But the Void Herald does not speak. It simply appears, watches, and vanishes. Unless, of course, it has been trying to communicate, and no one has been able to understand."
